---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const services = {
  'custom-software-development': {
    name: 'Custom Software Development', keyword: 'custom software development', title: 'Custom Software Development Services | VYQDA',
    description: 'Plan and build custom business software around your users, workflows, integrations and long-term maintenance needs.',
    intro: 'Custom software built around the work your business needs to do',
    opening: 'Off-the-shelf software can be a good fit when your process is standard. When the workflow, data model, approvals or user experience are specific to your organisation, custom software may be worth evaluating. VYQDA starts with the business problem and works toward a solution that can be understood, tested and maintained.',
    sections: [
      ['When custom development makes sense', 'Consider custom development when an important workflow cannot be handled well by existing tools, when several disconnected systems create avoidable manual work, or when the software itself is part of the product or service you offer. Before building, compare configuration, integration and process changes; writing new code is not always the simplest answer.'],
      ['From requirements to a useful scope', 'A useful brief describes the people involved, the current process, exceptions, data, permissions and the result that should improve. It should also identify constraints such as existing infrastructure, required integrations, budget range, timeline and who will make decisions. This allows a team to separate essential requirements from ideas that can wait.'],
      ['How delivery should work', 'Discovery and technical planning should surface risks early. Implementation should proceed in reviewable increments, with automated and manual testing appropriate to the system, clear acceptance criteria and documentation. Stakeholder reviews help ensure the delivered workflow reflects how people actually work rather than how the process looked in an initial diagram.'],
      ['Maintainability after launch', 'Software needs ownership after release. Plan for bug fixes, dependency updates, access management, backups where relevant, monitoring, documentation and future changes. The exact support arrangement should be explicit, including responsibilities, expected response times and any service hours.']
    ],
    goodFit: ['A business workflow is important but poorly served by existing products.', 'Different systems need to share information more reliably.', 'A customer-facing product needs a differentiated experience.', 'The organisation can provide a decision-maker and review feedback.'],
    notFit: ['A small process change or an existing product setting would solve the problem.', 'Requirements are unknown and nobody is available to clarify them.', 'The project assumes unconfirmed third-party access or undocumented APIs.']
  },
  'saas-product-development': {
    name: 'SaaS & Product Development', keyword: 'SaaS product development', title: 'SaaS & Product Development Services | VYQDA',
    description: 'SaaS product engineering from problem discovery and MVP scope through implementation, testing and continued iteration.',
    intro: 'Turn a clear product idea into software you can keep improving',
    opening: 'SaaS development is more than building a set of screens. Product teams must understand a target user, define the value proposition, decide what the first release must prove and plan for the product to evolve. VYQDA approaches product engineering with that wider lifecycle in mind.',
    sections: [
      ['Start with a real user problem', 'Describe the target user, the situation that triggers the need, current alternatives and why those alternatives are insufficient. These answers help decide whether to build a new product, integrate with existing tools or validate the idea with a smaller experiment before committing to a large scope.'],
      ['Define an MVP that tests a hypothesis', 'An MVP should support a complete, useful core journey—not merely contain fewer features. Prioritise the functionality required to deliver the promised value, and decide what evidence will guide the next iteration. Account roles, data boundaries, onboarding, error states and administration can be essential even when the first release is narrow.'],
      ['Plan the product foundation', 'The architecture should reflect expected workflows and realistic constraints without prematurely optimising for scale that has not been demonstrated. Discuss authentication, permissions, data model, billing dependencies, integrations, backups, logging and deployment responsibilities according to the product’s actual requirements.'],
      ['Iterate using feedback', 'After release, observe where users succeed or struggle, collect support feedback and prioritise improvements against product goals. Maintenance, security updates, documentation and clear ownership are ongoing responsibilities, not separate from product development.']
    ],
    goodFit: ['You have a defined user and problem to validate.', 'You need an MVP with a realistic path to iteration.', 'An existing product needs new workflows or technical improvements.', 'You can provide product decisions and timely feedback.'],
    notFit: ['The target user and problem are still entirely undefined.', 'Success is described only as a long list of features.', 'The project depends on guaranteed market adoption or revenue.']
  },
  'web-mobile-applications': {
    name: 'Web & Mobile Applications', keyword: 'web and mobile application development', title: 'Web & Mobile Application Development | VYQDA',
    description: 'User-focused web and mobile application development based on real journeys, accessibility, integration and operational needs.',
    intro: 'Web and mobile applications designed around real user journeys',
    opening: 'An application succeeds when users can complete the task they came to do. Interface design, system behaviour, accessibility, performance and integration all shape that experience. VYQDA scopes web and mobile application work around the users, journeys and operational conditions the product must support.',
    sections: [
      ['Map the user journey before designing screens', 'Identify entry points, common tasks, decision points, error conditions and what happens when a user leaves and returns. Include administrators and support staff as well as end users. A journey map helps reveal unnecessary steps and missing states before they become expensive to fix.'],
      ['Choose the right delivery approach', 'A responsive web application may be sufficient for workflows used through a browser. A mobile application may be justified when device capabilities, distribution, offline behaviour or frequent field use require it. The decision should follow user needs and technical constraints rather than assumptions about what every product must have.'],
      ['Design for reliability and accessibility', 'Forms, navigation, feedback messages, loading states and error handling should be understandable on small screens and with keyboard or assistive technology. Performance and accessibility need to be tested across representative devices and network conditions, not judged only from a developer workstation.'],
      ['Plan integration and release', 'List APIs, identity systems, data sources, notification channels and operational dependencies. Define test environments, release responsibilities, analytics requirements and how issues will be reported. Keep personal and sensitive data collection proportionate to the application’s purpose.']
    ],
    goodFit: ['Users need a clearer digital way to complete a recurring task.', 'A customer or partner journey needs to move online.', 'An existing application needs usability or workflow improvements.', 'The application must connect with existing business systems.'],
    notFit: ['A simple static page or existing platform feature already meets the need.', 'Device or integration requirements have not been investigated.', 'The project has no plan for user testing or ongoing ownership.']
  },
  'business-process-automation': {
    name: 'Business Process Automation', keyword: 'business process automation', title: 'Business Process Automation Services | VYQDA',
    description: 'Identify repeatable business workflows and plan automation with clear rules, exception handling, integrations and ownership.',
    intro: 'Automate repeatable work without losing control of the process',
    opening: 'Automation is most useful when the underlying process is understood. If a workflow contains unclear decisions, inconsistent data or frequent exceptions, automating it without redesign can simply make the confusion happen faster. VYQDA helps organisations map the process before deciding which steps should be automated.',
    sections: [
      ['Find the right workflow', 'Start with work that is repeated, rules-based and costly in staff time or error correction. Map the trigger, inputs, decisions, outputs, hand-offs and exceptions. Confirm how often the workflow occurs and what currently makes it slow or unreliable before estimating the value of automation.'],
      ['Define rules and exception handling', 'Document which decisions can be made automatically and which require human review. Plan what happens when data is missing, an integration is unavailable, a request is duplicated or an approval is overdue. Logging and clear ownership make the workflow easier to troubleshoot.'],
      ['Connect systems carefully', 'Automation may involve APIs, spreadsheets, email, databases or existing business applications. Confirm access rights, data formats, rate limits, retry behaviour and ownership of each system. Avoid collecting or copying more information than the process requires.'],
      ['Measure and maintain the process', 'Agree on a baseline and practical indicators such as processing time, rework, completion rate or exception volume when those measures can be collected reliably. Monitor failures, review rule changes and assign an owner for ongoing maintenance. Do not assume a projected time saving is an achieved result.']
    ],
    goodFit: ['The same workflow is repeated frequently with clear rules.', 'Manual hand-offs create delays or duplicate data entry.', 'Several systems need a controlled exchange of information.', 'Exceptions can be defined and routed to a responsible person.'],
    notFit: ['The process changes every time and has no agreed rules.', 'Data quality is poor and nobody owns its correction.', 'Automation would bypass necessary human judgement or approval.']
  },
  'maintenance-support': {
    name: 'Software Maintenance & Support', keyword: 'software maintenance and support', title: 'Software Maintenance & Support Services | VYQDA',
    description: 'Keep business software maintainable through planned fixes, dependency updates, technical improvements and ongoing development.',
    intro: 'Keep software useful after the initial release',
    opening: 'Software continues to need attention after launch. User needs change, dependencies are updated, integrations evolve and operational issues appear. A maintenance plan helps organisations decide what must be kept stable, what should be improved and how technical risks will be handled over time.',
    sections: [
      ['Understand the current system', 'Before changing an application, review its architecture, repository, deployment process, dependencies, data flows, known issues and existing documentation. Clarify ownership of hosting, credentials, third-party services and production access. This reduces the risk of making changes without understanding their effects.'],
      ['Separate urgent fixes from planned improvements', 'Classify issues by impact and urgency, reproduce problems where possible and agree on acceptance criteria for fixes. Keep feature requests and refactoring visible alongside defects so that short-term repairs do not silently create a growing maintenance burden.'],
      ['Protect release quality', 'Use a repeatable workflow for code review, automated checks, testing, environment configuration and release notes appropriate to the application. Backups, rollback plans and monitoring should be considered according to the system’s data and operational importance.'],
      ['Make support expectations explicit', 'Document communication channels, coverage, response expectations, escalation paths, included maintenance and out-of-scope work. Do not assume 24/7 availability or a guaranteed response time unless it is explicitly agreed in writing.']
    ],
    goodFit: ['An application needs regular fixes and dependency updates.', 'A product requires ongoing feature development.', 'Knowledge is concentrated in a few people and documentation is incomplete.', 'The business needs a clearer maintenance backlog and release process.'],
    notFit: ['No one can authorise access or explain the application’s purpose.', 'The system is expected to be changed without testing or a release plan.', 'Support expectations have not been agreed with the service provider.']
  }
  };
  return Object.entries(services).map(([service, data]) => ({ params: { service }, props: { data, service } }));
}
const { data, service } = Astro.props;
const canonical = `https://vyqda.com/services/${service}/`;
---
<SiteLayout title={data.title} description={data.description} canonical={canonical} active="services">
  <section class="page-hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">VYQDA DEVELOPMENT SERVICES</p><h1>{data.intro}</h1><p class="page-hero__lead">{data.opening}</p>
    <div class="page-hero__actions"><a class="button button--primary" href="/contact/#project-enquiry">Discuss your requirements <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/services/">All services</a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section wrap"><div class="content-section__intro"><p class="eyebrow">A PRACTICAL APPROACH</p><h2>Start with the work, then choose the technology</h2></div>
    <div class="content-grid content-grid--two">{data.sections.map(([heading, body], index) => <article class="content-card"><span class="content-card__index">0{index + 1}</span><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{body}</p></article>)}</div>
  </section>
  <section class="content-section content-section--tint"><div class="wrap content-section__split"><div><p class="eyebrow">FIT AND READINESS</p><h2>When this service may be a good fit</h2><p>Use these prompts to decide whether a discovery conversation is likely to be productive.</p></div><div><h3>Potential fit</h3><ul class="check-list">{data.goodFit.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul><h3>Resolve these issues first</h3><ul class="check-list">{data.notFit.map(item => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></div></div></section>
  <section class="content-section wrap"><div class="content-section__intro"><p class="eyebrow">RELATED WORK</p><h2>Connect the service to the wider project</h2><p>Some requirements are best handled as a focused improvement; others combine product thinking, integration and long-term engineering. Explore the related pages below or share the situation for a more specific discussion.</p></div>
    <div class="related-links"><a href="/services/">All development services <span>↗</span></a><a href="/solutions/">Business software solutions <span>↗</span></a><a href="/products/">VYQDA software products <span>↗</span></a><a href="/contact/#project-enquiry">Discuss your project <span>↗</span></a></div>
  </section>
  <section class="closing-cta wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow">DEFINE THE NEXT STEP</p><h2>What does your team need to improve?</h2><p>Share the current workflow, users, systems and constraints. A focused brief helps establish the right scope before development begins.</p></div><a class="button button--primary" href="/contact/#project-enquiry">Talk to VYQDA <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
</SiteLayout>
